  "title": "tolong, silakan, ayo, jangan",
  "pattern": "Tolong + verb · Silakan + verb · Ayo / Mari + verb · Jangan + verb",
  "explanation": "English 'please' splits in two. Tolong (literally 'help') asks someone to do something for you: Tolong buka pintunya (Please open the door). Silakan invites someone to do something for themselves: Silakan duduk (Please sit down), Silakan masuk (Come in). Ayo, or the more polite mari, is 'let's': Ayo makan! (Let's eat!). And jangan is 'don't': Jangan lupa (Don't forget).",
  "natural": "Adding ya at the end softens any of them: Tolong tunggu sebentar, ya (Wait a moment, please). Silakan is what hosts and staff say to you, as they offer a seat, food or the way in.",
  "stiff": "Silakan for your own request gets it backwards: it offers, it doesn't ask. To ask for something, use tolong for an action, or minta for a thing: Minta air putih, ya (Some water, please). A bare command with no tolong or ya, like Buka pintu!, sounds bossy.",
